---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="What Makes a Procurement Professional? | HYWSource"
  description="What really makes a procurement professional? Reflections on qualifications, practical experience, professional judgement and continual learning."
>
  <main class="article">
    <article>
      <header class="article-header">
        <h1>What Makes a Procurement Professional?</h1>
        <p class="intro">
          Professional qualifications, practical experience and the ability to
          make sound business decisions all play a role. But what should really
          define a procurement professional?
        </p>
      </header>

      <section>
        <p>
          I recently came across a discussion about what makes someone a
          procurement professional. One of the responses caught my attention.
          It suggested that the strongest professionals combine knowledge,
          experience, judgement and continual learning. More importantly, the
          real test should not be which route someone took to enter the
          profession, but what decisions they can make, how well they can
          justify those decisions, and what business outcomes they produce.
        </p>
        <p>I found myself agreeing with this point.</p>
        <p>
          It also made me think about my own career path and how I came to work
          in procurement and supply chain management.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>There Is More Than One Way Into Procurement</h2>
        <p>
          I did not start my career in procurement. My first role was in
          international trade sales, where I worked with overseas customers
          and manufacturers. At that stage, my understanding of the business
          was mainly from the sales and trade perspective.
        </p>
        <p>
          As my responsibilities developed, I became increasingly involved in
          sourcing, supplier management and supply chain operations. Over
          time, procurement became a much bigger part of my work.
        </p>
        <p>
          My route into procurement was therefore quite different from someone
          who studied procurement or supply chain management from the
          beginning.
        </p>
        <p>
          Later, I decided to study for the CIPS qualifications. I wanted to
          build a more structured understanding of procurement, rather than
          relying only on what I had learned through work. I studied from
          Level 4 through to Level 6 while continuing to develop my practical
          experience.
        </p>
        <p>
          Looking back, both parts of that journey were important. My work
          experience helped me understand the real challenges businesses and
          suppliers face. CIPS gave me a more systematic way to understand
          procurement principles, evaluate different approaches and connect
          day-to-day decisions with wider business objectives.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is one reason I do not believe there is only one correct route
          to becoming a procurement professional.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Does a Professional Qualification Make Someone a Professional?</h2>
        <p>
          Professional qualifications such as CIPS are valuable. They provide
          a structured framework for learning procurement principles,
          processes and practices. They can also help professionals identify
          gaps in their knowledge and develop a broader understanding of the
          function.
        </p>
        <p>
          However, holding a qualification does not automatically mean someone
          can handle every practical procurement situation.
        </p>
        <p>
          Knowing the principles of strategic sourcing, for example, is
          different from developing and implementing a sourcing strategy when
          suppliers have different capabilities, costs, risks and commercial
          interests.
        </p>
        <p>
          Understanding supplier relationship management in theory is also
          different from managing a supplier through quality problems,
          production delays or difficult commercial discussions.
        </p>
        <p>
          At the same time, I would not say that someone without a professional
          qualification cannot be a procurement professional.
        </p>
        <p>
          There are people who have built substantial procurement expertise
          through years of practical work. They may not hold CIPS or another
          formal qualification, but they understand suppliers, markets, costs,
          production, quality and delivery. They can assess situations, make
          independent decisions and explain the reasons behind those
          decisions.
        </p>
        <p>
          Their professional capability should not be dismissed simply because
          they followed a different learning path.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Experience Matters, but Experience Alone Is Not Enough</h2>
        <p>
          I also think it is important not to treat years of experience as
          proof of competence.
        </p>
        <p>
          Someone may have worked in procurement for fifteen years, but that
          does not necessarily mean they have developed the same level of
          professional judgement as someone with fewer years of experience.
        </p>
        <p>Experience becomes valuable when we learn from it.</p>
        <p>
          In procurement, we regularly face situations where there is no
          straightforward answer. A supplier may offer a lower price, but can
          they maintain the required quality and delivery performance? A
          supplier may promise sufficient production capacity, but does the
          actual production process support that promise? Should we continue
          negotiating with an existing supplier, or is it time to develop an
          alternative source?
        </p>
        <p>
          These decisions require more than following procedures or relying
          on what worked in the past.
        </p>
        <p>
          They require us to understand the business requirements, assess the
          available information, consider the risks and make a decision that
          can be justified.
        </p>
        <p>
          I believe this is where professional judgement becomes particularly
          important.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>What Should We Really Look at When Assessing a Procurement Professional?</h2>
        <p>
          For me, it comes down to several connected capabilities.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Knowledge:</strong> Do they understand procurement
            principles, sourcing strategies, supplier management, cost
            analysis, contracts and supply chain risks?
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Experience:</strong> Have they dealt with real suppliers,
            production issues, quality concerns, delivery challenges and
            commercial negotiations?
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Judgement:</strong> Can they assess different options,
            recognise risks and make sound decisions when information is
            incomplete or circumstances change?
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Accountability:</strong> Can they explain why a decision
            was made and take responsibility for its consequences?
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Continual learning:</strong> Are they willing to review
            their own decisions, learn from mistakes and keep their knowledge
            relevant as the business environment changes?
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          These capabilities do not always come from the same place. Some may
          be developed through formal education, some through practical work,
          and others through reflection and continued learning.
        </p>
        <p>They should complement one another.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Procurement Is About Business Decisions, Not Just Purchasing Tasks</h2>
        <p>
          Another point worth considering is how procurement itself is
          understood within a business.
        </p>
        <p>
          If procurement is viewed only as finding suppliers, comparing
          quotations, placing orders and negotiating prices, then the value of
          the profession is easily underestimated.
        </p>
        <p>
          In practice, procurement decisions can affect product quality,
          production continuity, inventory, cash flow, delivery performance
          and the overall competitiveness of a business.
        </p>
        <p>
          Choosing the lowest-priced supplier is not always the best
          commercial decision. Increasing order volume with one supplier may
          reduce unit cost, but it may also increase supply risk. A supplier's
          quoted production capacity may look sufficient on paper, but the
          actual production process may tell a different story.
        </p>
        <p>
          A procurement professional needs to look beyond the immediate
          transaction and understand the wider business impact.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is also why I believe the quality of a decision matters, not
          just whether the final outcome was successful. External factors can
          affect even a well-considered decision. What matters is whether the
          professional assessed the situation properly, considered the risks,
          made a reasonable choice and learned from the result.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Qualifications, Experience and Continuous Development Should Work Together</h2>
        <p>
          I do not see professional qualifications and practical experience
          as competing alternatives.
        </p>
        <p>
          A qualification can provide structure to what we know and challenge
          us to think beyond our existing experience. Practical work shows us
          how those principles apply in real situations, where business
          priorities, supplier capabilities and unexpected problems rarely
          fit neatly into a textbook.
        </p>
        <p>For me, the value comes from bringing these together.</p>
        <p>
          Some procurement professionals enter the field through formal
          study. Others develop their expertise through years of hands-on
          work. Some, like me, move into procurement from another business
          function and build their professional knowledge along the way.
        </p>
        <p>
          The route may be different, but the responsibility is the same:
          to make sound decisions, understand their business impact and keep
          improving.
        </p>
        <p>
          Ultimately, I believe a procurement professional should be judged
          less by the qualification they hold or the number of years they
          have worked, and more by the knowledge they apply, the decisions
          they make and the value they bring to the business.
        </p>
        <p class="closing">
          There is more than one way to become a procurement professional.
          What matters is the professional capability you build and
          demonstrate along the way.
        </p>
      </section>
    </article>
  </main>
</Layout>

